
# ── Journey 7: Professional Presence ────────────────────────────────────────
levels.append({'id':61,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'First Impressions',
'scenario':'You have been invited to a senior industry roundtable — a small gathering of ten leaders from your sector. You are the most junior person in the room. You want to make a strong first impression not by trying to impress but by being genuinely present, confident, and interesting to talk to.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You arrive five minutes early and there are already several people in the room. How do you enter?',
'Walk in with composure, scan the room briefly, make eye contact with the person nearest to you, smile, and introduce yourself: "Hi — I\'m [Name]. Great to be here."',
['Find a quiet corner and check your phone while waiting for others to approach you.','Immediately approach the most senior person in the room to introduce yourself.','Wait near the door until the organiser invites you to join the group.'],
'Confident entry, warm eye contact, and a simple self-introduction to whoever is nearest signals social ease and initiative. You do not need to target the most senior person first.',
'Retreating to a corner with your phone signals social discomfort or disengagement. Approaching the most senior person immediately can feel calculated. Waiting at the door for the organiser signals passivity.'),
mcq(2,'A senior leader, Diane, introduces herself and asks: "So, what brings you to this roundtable?" How do you answer in a way that is confident and interesting?',
'"I\'m at [Company], leading [specific initiative]. We\'re at an interesting moment — [one compelling specific detail] — and I wanted to hear how others in the sector are thinking about it."',
['"Oh, I was invited — I\'m not sure I quite belong here yet, honestly."','"I\'m just starting out really — I\'m here to learn more than anything."','"I\'m at [Company] — you might have heard of us. We\'re quite well known in the space."'],
'A confident, specific answer that names your context and a specific interesting detail about your current moment makes you immediately memorable and opens a natural conversation.',
'"I\'m not sure I quite belong here" is self-deprecating in a way that puts the awkwardness on Diane. "Here to learn more than anything" is humble but undersells your presence. Invoking your company\'s reputation to name-drop is arrogant.'),
mcq(3,'Diane says, "That\'s interesting — we\'ve been grappling with something similar." She pauses. How do you respond?',
'Lean in slightly and say: "Really? I\'d love to hear how you\'re approaching it — what\'s been the biggest tension point for you?"',
['Immediately tell Diane everything you have learned about the topic in your organisation.','Nod and say, "Hmm, interesting" without following up.','Say, "You probably have more experience with it than we do — what do you think I should do?"'],
'A brief, genuine expression of interest and a specific follow-up question — what has been the biggest tension point — turns a social exchange into a real conversation and shows you are curious, not performing.',
'Downloading everything you know about the topic is a monologue rather than a dialogue. Nodding without following up closes the conversational door she just opened. Immediately deferring to her experience and asking for advice positions you as a student rather than a peer.'),
mcq(4,'The roundtable formally begins. The facilitator asks everyone to share one challenge they are currently navigating. When it is your turn, how do you contribute?',
'State your challenge clearly and specifically in three sentences. End with: "I\'m genuinely curious whether others have navigated this."',
['Say, "I\'m not sure my challenges are quite at the level of everyone else\'s here..."','Give a lengthy explanation of your challenge with extensive background.','Echo someone else\'s challenge slightly and add, "Something similar for us."'],
'Clarity, specificity, and an invitation for collective engagement — "I\'m genuinely curious whether others have navigated this" — positions you as both confident and collaborative.',
'"Not quite at the level of everyone else\'s" undermines your position before you have said anything. A lengthy explanation with extensive background loses the room. Echoing someone else\'s challenge rather than naming your own suggests you were not prepared or did not want to take the risk.'),
mcq(5,'After the roundtable, a senior leader says, "I\'d like to continue our conversation — can I have your card?" How do you close this moment?',
'Provide your card or contact, make direct eye contact, and say: "I would genuinely like that — your perspective on [specific topic from the conversation] was exactly the kind of thinking I was hoping to encounter today."',
['Give your card and say, "Of course — call anytime!"','Get nervous and apologise for not having a business card.','Say, "Sure — though I\'m not sure how much I can add for someone at your level."'],
'Accepting with warmth and naming the specific part of the conversation that made it valuable signals that you were genuinely present and pays a specific, earned compliment.',
'"Call anytime" is casual and non-committal. Apologising for not having a card without offering an alternative is a weak close. "Not sure how much I can add for someone at your level" is self-deprecating at the exact wrong moment.')],
'response':resp('You entered with composure, gave a confident and specific answer about your work, asked a strong follow-up question, contributed a clear challenge with an invitation for engagement, and closed with a specific and genuine note of appreciation. Now type or say aloud the follow-up email you would send Diane within 24 hours of the roundtable.',
'Excellent! First impressions are not made in a single moment — they are built across every small choice in a conversation: how you enter, how you listen, how you ask, how you close. You made all of them well today.')})

levels.append({'id':62,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Commanding a Room',
'scenario':'You are presenting the quarterly business review to a room of thirty colleagues, including several senior leaders. It is your most important presentation of the year. You know your content well, but you want to project genuine authority and command the room — not just deliver information.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You walk to the front of the room. Before you speak, what do you do?',
'Stand still, plant your feet, make a slow sweep of eye contact across the room, and wait for full attention before you begin.',
['Start speaking immediately before people have fully settled so you seem eager.','Arrange your notes or adjust the slides first to settle yourself.','Grip the lectern firmly with both hands to steady yourself.'],
'Standing still and making deliberate eye contact before speaking is one of the most powerful things a presenter can do. It commands attention through silence rather than words.',
'Starting before the room settles means you begin with people still distracted. Adjusting notes or slides while the room waits signals that you are not fully ready. Gripping the lectern makes you look anchored to it and physically constrained.'),
mcq(2,'You open with your first line. Which option commands the room?',
'"In the last quarter, we grew revenue by 12% in a market that contracted by 4%. I want to tell you how that happened and what it means for the next twelve months."',
['"Good morning everyone — I\'ll be presenting our Q3 results today and running through the key highlights."','Start with a long thank-you to everyone who prepared the presentation.','Open with a joke about how long the meeting will take.'],
'A compelling data point and a clear narrative promise — how it happened, what it means — is the opening that makes the room lean in immediately.',
'"Good morning — I\'ll be presenting" is a procedural opening that does nothing to engage. A long thank-you delays the content and reduces your authority. A joke about the meeting length signals that you too would rather be elsewhere.'),
mcq(3,'Mid-presentation, you notice some people in the back row are looking at their phones. How do you bring them back without losing the flow?',
'Pause for a deliberate beat, move slightly toward the back of the room if possible, and speak your next line directly toward that section of the audience.',
['Raise your voice to project more energy toward the back.','Say, "I hope those of you at the back can hear me."','Ignore it and continue — it is not worth interrupting the flow.'],
'Physical movement toward an area of disengagement naturally draws attention there. A deliberate pause before a key point resets the room\'s focus without calling anyone out.',
'Raising your voice signals that you noticed and are trying to compete with their distraction. "I hope those of you at the back can hear me" calls out the section in a slightly passive way. Ignoring it allows the disengagement to persist and potentially spread.'),
mcq(4,'A senior leader interrupts with a challenging question mid-presentation. How do you respond?',
'Stop, hold the question with a brief nod, and say: "That\'s the right question — let me address it now rather than at the end." Then answer it confidently and transition back: "That brings me to the next point."',
['Say, "I\'ll get to that at the end — can you hold the question?"','Get flustered and lose your place: "Uh — good point, let me just find where I was..."','Spend so long on the answer that the presentation runs out of time.'],
'Welcoming the interruption, handling it with authority, and using it as a transition signals total command of both the material and the room.',
'"Hold the question" can feel slightly controlling with a senior leader. Getting flustered and losing your place exposes your anxiety. Spending so long on the answer that you run out of time shows poor time management.'),
mcq(5,'You reach your final slide. How do you close in a way that commands the room to the very last second?',
'Remove the final slide, stand in front of the screen or step to the centre of the room, make eye contact with the senior leaders, and say: "We grew in a contracting market. The plan for the next twelve months builds on exactly that. I\'m confident in it — and I welcome your questions."',
['Say, "And that\'s it from me — any questions?" somewhat apologetically.','Go back through the key points as a summary at the end of an already long presentation.','Wait for the facilitator to close for you.'],
'Removing the slide distraction, centering your physical presence, making specific eye contact, and ending with a confident declaration followed by an invitation to engage is a commanding close.',
'"And that\'s it from me — any questions?" is self-effacing at the moment you should be strongest. A summary at the end of a long presentation tests everyone\'s patience. Waiting for the facilitator hands away the authority you have spent the whole presentation building.')],
'response':resp('You entered with deliberate stillness, opened with a compelling data narrative, used movement to re-engage a distracted section, welcomed an interruption with authority, and closed with your physical presence and a confident declaration. Now type or say aloud the one-sentence closing statement you would use for this specific quarterly review.',
'Outstanding! Commanding a room is not about being loud or dominant — it is about being fully present, physically and verbally, in every moment. Stillness, movement, eye contact, and deliberate silence are your most powerful tools. You used them all.')})

levels.append({'id':63,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Projecting Confidence',
'scenario':'You have been offered a stretch assignment — leading a project significantly above your current level. You believe you can do it but you are experiencing real self-doubt. A mentor advises you that much of your success will depend not just on your capability but on how confidently you carry yourself with the senior stakeholders. You decide to work on projecting confidence even when you do not fully feel it.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'In your first stakeholder meeting, a senior leader asks, "Do you have experience running a project at this scale?" How do you answer honestly and confidently?',
'"Not at exactly this scale — but I\'ve led [specific comparable experience] and I\'ve studied the elements of this project carefully. I\'m bringing structured thinking and strong support around me. I\'m confident this is deliverable."',
['"Honestly, no — this is a bit of a stretch for me."','Overclaim: "Yes, absolutely — I\'ve done similar things many times."','Say, "I\'m still getting up to speed on some elements — bear with me."'],
'A honest admission of the gap — not at this exact scale — paired with a specific comparison, a named competency, and a clear expression of confidence is the ideal balance.',
'"This is a bit of a stretch" is honest but lowers confidence before you have begun. Overclaiming erodes trust the moment it is tested. "Bear with me" positions you as someone who needs tolerance, not a leader who is ready.'),
mcq(2,'A stakeholder challenges one of your assumptions. You are not fully sure she is wrong. How do you respond confidently without bluffing?',
'"That\'s a fair challenge — let me look at it more carefully before I commit to the assumption. I\'d rather we build on solid ground. Can I come back to you by Wednesday with a clear answer?"',
['Double down: "No, I\'m comfortable with the assumption — let\'s proceed."','Say, "You may be right — I\'ll change it to your version."','Get defensive: "The assumption was reviewed before we started — it should be fine."'],
'Acknowledging the challenge, refusing to bluff, and committing to a specific follow-up by a specific date is the most confident and honest response — confidence includes knowing what you do not yet know.',
'Doubling down on an assumption you are uncertain about is a risk that can damage your credibility badly later. Immediately deferring to her version signals that your positions are not based on conviction. Defensiveness about a pre-existing assumption that may be wrong signals insecurity.'),
mcq(3,'Before a large presentation, you feel a surge of anxiety. What do you do?',
'In the two minutes before walking in: stand upright, take five slow deep breaths, remind yourself of one specific thing you know deeply about this project, and walk in.',
['Try to suppress the anxiety by telling yourself to stop feeling it.','Apologise in advance: "I should warn you — I\'m a little nervous today."','Lean heavily on your slides so you can read from them if the anxiety hits.'],
'Physiological regulation — breathing, posture — combined with an anchor to specific knowledge you have confidence in, are the two most effective pre-performance tools.',
'Suppressing emotion does not reduce it — it intensifies it. Apologising in advance for nerves reduces your authority before you begin. Leaning on slides to read from them in a moment of anxiety signals lack of preparation and further undermines confidence.'),
mcq(4,'A stakeholder says, "How are you feeling about the timeline?" They are testing your confidence, not just asking for data. How do you respond?',
'"I\'m confident in the timeline. We have built in contingencies for the two highest-risk elements. It is tight — and we are managing it actively."',
['"Honestly, I have some concerns about it — it\'s ambitious."','Say, "Ask me in three weeks — I\'ll have a better answer then."','List all the risks to the timeline to demonstrate that you have thought about it.'],
'Directly naming your confidence, stating the specific risk mitigation already in place, and naming the tightness without apology is a confident, credible answer.',
'"I have some concerns" when directly asked if you are confident is an honest answer that also reduces stakeholder confidence in your leadership. "Ask me in three weeks" defers when a clear answer is needed now. Listing all the risks sounds like a qualification of the timeline rather than a confident response.'),
mcq(5,'At the project review, a senior leader says, "You\'ve handled this more capably than we expected at this level." How do you receive this?',
'"Thank you — I appreciate that. The team around me has been exceptional and I\'ve worked hard to stay ahead of the risk. I\'m excited about where we\'re taking this in the next phase."',
['"Oh, I\'m sure I had a lot of luck — it could easily have gone differently."','"Thank you! I\'m honestly surprised myself — it\'s been a real stretch."','Say, "I\'m glad it\'s come across that way" and quickly change the subject.'],
'Receiving the compliment graciously, distributing credit to the team, acknowledging your own effort, and pivoting forward is the confident, professional response.',
'"A lot of luck" attributes your success to chance and invites doubt. "I\'m surprised myself — it\'s been a stretch" confirms the initial uncertainty they may have had. "I\'m glad it\'s come across that way" implies it is a performance rather than a reality.')],
'response':resp('You answered capability questions honestly with a specific comparison and clear confidence, refused to bluff under challenge, used physiological tools to manage anxiety, responded to timeline pressure with specific mitigation, and received a compliment with genuine grace. Now type or say aloud the journal entry or private note you would write to yourself at the end of this project about what you learned about confidence.',
'Brilliant! Confidence is not the absence of doubt — it is the decision to lead despite it. You showed that honest, specific, composure-supported presence can fill a room even when the room feels too big. That is what growing into a role looks like.')})

levels.append({'id':64,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Managing Your Reputation',
'scenario':'You have learned through a trusted colleague that there is a perception in parts of your organisation that you are "brilliant but difficult to work with." You are shocked — this is not how you see yourself. But you take it seriously. You want to understand where this is coming from and manage your professional reputation proactively.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Your colleague has shared the feedback. How do you respond to the disclosure?',
'"Thank you for telling me — I know it wasn\'t easy to bring this. I want to understand it rather than dismiss it. Can you tell me more about where you heard it and what specifically people mean by difficult?"',
['Get defensive: "That\'s just not accurate — I get results and people know that."','Say, "I\'m not going to worry about what people say behind my back."','Immediately start listing the people you think might be saying this.'],
'Thanking the colleague for the courage to share, committing to understand rather than dismiss, and asking for specificity are exactly the right moves in response to reputational feedback.',
'Defending yourself by citing your results implicitly dismisses the feedback. "I\'m not going to worry about it" signals that you are not taking the information seriously. Immediately naming possible sources turns a self-development moment into a blame hunt.'),
mcq(2,'Your colleague says, "People feel like you can be dismissive when your ideas are challenged." How do you respond?',
'"That\'s hard to hear — and I think I know what moments they might be referring to. I can get impatient when I think we\'re going in the wrong direction. I can see how that might land as dismissive."',
['"I challenge ideas because I care about the outcome — surely that\'s a good thing?"','Say, "I think some people just can\'t handle directness."','Stay silent to process without saying anything out loud.'],
'Acknowledging the feedback, identifying the specific internal driver — impatience — and seeing how it lands from the outside is genuine self-reflection that turns the feedback into something actionable.',
'"I challenge ideas because I care" reframes the behaviour as a virtue without examining the impact. "Some people can\'t handle directness" is a defensive attribution that blames the receivers. Staying completely silent gives your colleague nothing to indicate you are processing the information.'),
mcq(3,'You want to get a clearer picture. Who do you speak to next?',
'Ask two or three trusted colleagues whom you interact with frequently to give you honest feedback on how you come across in collaborative moments.',
['Go to your manager and ask for the names of the people saying these things.','Say nothing and observe how people react to you over the next month without asking.','Post an anonymous survey to your team asking for feedback on your style.'],
'A small number of trusted colleagues who can give you honest, firsthand feedback on your specific collaborative behaviour is the most useful source of direct information.',
'Asking for names from your manager creates a witch-hunt atmosphere and may put your manager in a difficult position. Observing passively for a month without asking gives you no direct information. An anonymous survey to your whole team is a large and formal response to what should begin as a quiet, personal investigation.'),
mcq(4,'A trusted colleague tells you he has noticed you sometimes talk over people in meetings when you are excited about a topic. How do you respond?',
'"Thank you — I can absolutely see that. When I\'m energised about a topic I do speed up and I can cut across people. That\'s something I want to actively work on. Can I ask you to flag it if you see it happen?"',
['Say, "I don\'t mean to — people know I\'m just enthusiastic."','"Maybe — but I also think people need to be more assertive if they want to be heard."','Get upset: "I\'m trying so hard and apparently nothing I do is right."'],
'Acknowledging the specific behaviour — talking over people when excited — naming the internal driver, committing to work on it, and asking for an accountability partner is exactly the right response.',
'"People know I\'m just enthusiastic" is an intent-based excuse that does not address the impact. "People need to be more assertive" makes others responsible for your behaviour. Getting upset and catastrophising makes the feedback conversation about your emotional state.'),
mcq(5,'Three months later, your colleague says: "I don\'t know what you did but people\'s perception of you has genuinely shifted. You seem more open in meetings." How do you respond?',
'"I worked on it deliberately — and I had some help from people like you who were honest with me. I\'m glad it shows."',
['Say, "Wow — really? I wasn\'t sure if people would notice."','Say, "I just tried to be myself — sometimes perception is unfair."','Dismiss it: "It\'s probably just because we\'ve had some good outcomes recently."'],
'Acknowledging the deliberate effort, crediting the people who helped with honest feedback, and receiving the observation warmly is genuinely confident and honest.',
'"Wasn\'t sure if people would notice" undersells the intentionality of the change. "Just tried to be myself" misrepresents the deliberate behavioural work you did. Attributing the perception change to outcomes rather than behaviour suggests you do not fully own the change.')],
'response':resp('You received reputational feedback with openness, identified the specific behaviour and its driver, sought direct feedback from trusted colleagues, acknowledged a specific observed behaviour with accountability, and received confirmation of change graciously. Now type or say aloud one specific thing you would continue doing every week to protect and build your reputation going forward.',
'Remarkable! Managing your reputation is not about spin or image management — it is about taking honest feedback seriously and making real changes. You did the harder thing: you looked inward, sought more data, and changed specific behaviour. That is professional integrity.')})

levels.append({'id':65,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Networking with Purpose',
'scenario':'You are attending a professional conference with eight hundred attendees. You are an introvert and large events drain you. But you have set a clear intention: to leave this conference with three genuine new connections — not business cards, but real conversations that lead somewhere. You have four hours and you plan to be deliberate about it.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You arrive at the conference and the hall is buzzing with conversation. How do you start?',
'Look for someone standing alone or on the edge of a conversation group, walk over, make eye contact, smile, and say: "Hi — [Name]. Great conference so far."',
['Walk to the most crowded group and try to edge your way in.','Head straight to the refreshments table and wait for people to come to you.','Read the speaker bios on your phone until you feel ready.'],
'Finding someone who looks similarly hesitant or detached and approaching directly is the lowest-friction, highest-value opening move. You both benefit.',
'Edging into a crowded group is socially difficult and unlikely to produce a real exchange. Waiting at the refreshments table puts you in a passive position. Reading bios on your phone is a form of avoidance.'),
mcq(2,'The person you approached says, "I\'m Aisha — I\'m trying to figure out what sessions to prioritise. Do you have a view?" How do you respond in a way that is engaging and invites conversation?',
'"I\'ve been debating between the morning keynote and the AI applications track — mostly because [specific reason]. What are you here trying to get out of today?"',
['"I don\'t know — I\'m still figuring it out myself."','Recommend the sessions you plan to attend without asking her back.','Say, "I usually just go to all of them — you never know what will be good."'],
'Sharing a specific, honest view and immediately returning the question — "what are you trying to get out of today?" — turns a logistics question into the beginning of a real conversation.',
'"Still figuring it out" closes down the exchange. Recommending without asking her back makes it one-directional. "I usually go to all of them" is non-committal and offers her nothing specific to engage with.'),
mcq(3,'Aisha shares she is trying to understand how AI is reshaping her sector. It turns out you have direct experience with exactly that. How do you share it without dominating the conversation?',
'Share one specific insight in two or three sentences, then say: "We\'ve found X to be the most surprising thing — has your organisation started grappling with any of that?"',
['Share everything you know about AI in your sector for the next ten minutes.','Be modest: "I\'m not really an expert — I\'ve just been close to it."','Mention your company\'s specific use case and quickly pivot to asking about hers.'],
'A brief, specific insight followed by a question that returns to her experience is the ideal exchange rhythm: you offer value, you invite reciprocation.',
'Sharing everything you know is a monologue that disengages rather than connects. "Not really an expert" when you clearly have direct experience is false modesty. Quickly pivoting back after a mention is fine but "two or three sentences plus a question" is a cleaner, more generous rhythm.'),
mcq(4,'The session bell rings and you have to part ways. How do you close this first conversation?',
'"This has been genuinely useful — I\'d love to continue it. Would you be open to connecting on LinkedIn? I\'ll drop you a message referencing what we talked about."',
['Say, "Here\'s my card — let\'s stay in touch somehow."','Say, "Great to meet you" and walk away without asking for a follow-up.','Ask Aisha for her email, phone, and company so you can "loop her in" to your network.'],
'A specific platform, a commitment to a personalised follow-up, and a reason for connecting — the conversation you just had — is the most effective close.',
'A card without a follow-up plan is a card that gets lost. Walking away without a follow-up makes the conversation a dead end. Collecting all contact details at once feels like data harvesting rather than connection.'),
mcq(5,'At the end of the day you have had three strong conversations including Aisha\'s. You are exhausted. What do you do before you leave?',
'Find a quiet spot, write a one-line note about each person and the specific thing you want to follow up on — while it is still fresh.',
['Go home and plan to follow up tomorrow when you are less tired.','Send all three contacts a generic "great to meet you today!" message.','Review the full conference programme to see what you missed.'],
'Capturing the specific follow-up for each person while the conversation is fresh — before exhaustion erases the detail — is the difference between good intentions and real follow-through.',
'Waiting until tomorrow when you are "less tired" risks the details fading. A generic "great to meet you" message is the networking equivalent of a business card without a follow-up plan. Reviewing the programme at the end of the day is looking backward rather than securing the forward momentum you have built.')],
'response':resp('You found an approachable person, gave a specific view and returned the question, offered a brief insight followed by an invitation for her experience, closed with a specific platform and commitment, and captured follow-up notes before you left. Now type or say aloud the personalised follow-up message you would send Aisha on LinkedIn the next morning.',
'Superb! Networking with purpose is not about collecting contacts — it is about starting real conversations. You had three of those today. That is more valuable than fifty business cards.')})

levels.append({'id':66,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Personal Brand',
'scenario':'You have recently started a new role and you want to be intentional about how you show up and what you become known for. You want to build a personal brand — not as a performance, but as a consistent, authentic expression of your real strengths and values — that will serve your professional development over the long term.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Your manager asks all new team members to introduce themselves at the all-hands meeting. How do you frame your introduction to begin building your brand?',
'"I\'m [Name]. I\'m joining from [background]. What I care about most professionally is [specific value or strength — e.g. \'building things that actually get used\' or \'making complex things simple\']. I\'m looking forward to bringing that here."',
['"I\'m [Name]. I come from [background] and I\'ve done [list of past roles and companies]."','Say, "I\'m [Name] and I\'m really excited to be here — I just want to contribute wherever I can."','Give a brief funny anecdote about yourself to seem likeable and approachable.'],
'Naming a specific professional value — not just a history — immediately tells people what to expect from you and begins the process of brand-building.',
'A list of past roles and companies is a resume, not an introduction. "Wherever I can" is too general to build a distinct impression. An anecdote can work but is riskier and does not name the professional value you want to be known for.'),
mcq(2,'You want to identify two or three things you want to be known for. How do you figure this out?',
'Ask three people who know your work well: "What would you say I do consistently better than most people?" — and look for the overlap in their answers.',
['Choose the qualities that sound most impressive in your sector.','Mirror what the most successful people in your new team seem to be known for.','Pick qualities you aspire to have, even if you are not yet consistently demonstrating them.'],
'Asking for specific, firsthand feedback from people who have seen your work produces an accurate and evidence-based self-understanding that you can genuinely own.',
'Choosing impressive-sounding qualities without grounding in reality produces a brand that feels inauthentic. Mirroring others limits your distinctiveness. Picking aspirational qualities you do not yet consistently demonstrate creates a gap between your brand and your reality.'),
mcq(3,'A colleague asks your opinion on a piece of work in your area of strength. How do you respond in a way that advances your brand?',
'Give a clear, specific, honest response — naming what is strong, what could be better, and why — without hedging or performing false modesty.',
['Give vague, positive feedback to seem easy to work with.','Say, "I\'m not the expert here — what do you think?"','Give extensive critical feedback that covers every possible improvement.'],
'Clear, specific, honest opinions in your area of strength is how your brand becomes real rather than claimed. Over time, people seek out the person who gives them this.',
'Vague positive feedback in your area of strength is a missed opportunity and does not build a reputation for substantive thinking. "I\'m not the expert" when you are contradicts the brand you are trying to build. Extensive criticism without balance makes you known as critical rather than insightful.'),
mcq(4,'You discover that a piece of work you produced is being cited by a senior leader in a different department. How do you use this moment?',
'Send a brief note to the senior leader: "I heard my analysis on [topic] came up in your team — happy to walk through the methodology or explore the question further if it would be useful."',
['Do nothing — it will naturally spread your reputation without you needing to act.','Tell your immediate colleagues that your work is being used at a senior level.','Reach out to the senior leader\'s entire team to introduce yourself.'],
'Proactively connecting with the senior leader — with a specific offer — turns an organic moment of visibility into a real relationship.',
'Doing nothing allows the moment to pass without becoming a relationship. Telling your immediate colleagues is at best good news to share, but does nothing to advance the external relationship. Reaching out to the entire team is an overstep from a single introduction.'),
mcq(5,'A year into the role, a colleague says, "You\'re the person people go to when they need someone to cut through complexity." How do you respond?',
'"That\'s genuinely what I aim for — thank you for saying that. I want to keep earning that."',
['Say, "Oh I don\'t know about that — lots of people do that here."','"Really? That\'s nice — I hadn\'t thought about it that way."','Say, "I hope so — I work hard on it."'],
'Receiving the observation directly, affirming that it is intentional, and committing to keep earning it is genuinely confident and closes the loop on a year of deliberate brand-building.',
'False modesty undercuts a year of deliberate effort. "I hadn\'t thought about it that way" suggests the brand happened by accident. "I hope so — I work hard on it" is closer but slightly anxious in tone.')],
'response':resp('You framed your introduction around a specific professional value, grounded your brand in real evidence from trusted colleagues, gave specific honest feedback in your area of strength, turned a moment of visibility into a real relationship, and received a brand observation with confident ownership. Now type or say aloud the three-word phrase that best captures what you want to be known for professionally.',
'Outstanding! A personal brand built on real strengths, consistently demonstrated over time, and expressed with genuine honesty is one of the most durable professional assets you can have. You built one intentionally, not accidentally. That is what makes it real.')})

levels.append({'id':67,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Executive Communication',
'scenario':'You are preparing for your first presentation to the company\'s executive committee. You are presenting a recommendation to invest in a new capability. You know your material deeply but this audience is different from any you have addressed before. Executives want brevity, confidence, and clear recommendations — not detailed analysis.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You are designing your executive presentation. How many slides should your deck have?',
'No more than eight slides, with the recommendation and financial impact on slide one.',
['Twenty or more slides to demonstrate thoroughness and cover all scenarios.','A ten-to-twelve page detailed analysis document with slides for each analysis.','No slides — just talk through it naturally in a conversational format.'],
'Executives expect brevity and lead-with-the-conclusion structure. Eight or fewer slides with the recommendation on the first slide is the gold standard.',
'Twenty-plus slides signals that you have not done the work of prioritising. A detailed analysis document is an attachment to read later, not a presentation to navigate in real time. No slides at all is occasionally powerful but for a recommendation requiring approval, a structured summary is usually expected.'),
mcq(2,'You open the meeting. What is the first thing you say?',
'"I\'m here to recommend that we invest X in capability Y. My recommendation is yes, and here\'s the rationale in three points."',
['"I\'ll be presenting today on the opportunity to potentially invest in a new capability, depending on how the group feels about the risk."','"Before I get to the recommendation, let me give you some background on how we got here."','"I\'m not sure how much time we have today — how would you like to structure this?"'],
'Leading with a clear, unambiguous recommendation is exactly what executives need. "My recommendation is yes" removes any ambiguity about what you are asking for.',
'"Potentially depending on how the group feels" turns a recommendation into a temperature check. "Before I get to the recommendation, let me give you background" makes them wait for the most important information. "I\'m not sure how much time we have" signals a lack of preparation.'),
mcq(3,'The CFO interrupts: "What is the payback period on this investment?" You know the answer. How do you respond?',
'"Three years on the conservative model, eighteen months on the base case. The assumptions are on slide four if you want to stress-test them."',
['Get flustered: "Let me just find that — it\'s in the deck somewhere..."','Say, "I\'ll have my team send you the full financial model after the meeting."','Say, "It depends on a number of factors — can I come back to that?"'],
'A direct, precise answer with a reference to where the underlying assumptions are — without hesitation — signals full command of your material.',
'Hunting through the deck signals that you do not know your own numbers. Sending the model afterwards defers the answer the CFO needs now. "It depends" on a direct financial question from a CFO is an evasion.'),
mcq(4,'The CEO asks, "What is your biggest risk?" How do you answer with confidence?',
'"The biggest risk is adoption speed in the first six months — if we do not achieve X% usage by month six, the economic model weakens. We have a specific plan to address that, which I\'ll show you on slide six."',
['Say, "There are several risks — I\'ve listed them all on slide seven."','Say, "Honestly, I\'m not too worried — the business case is strong."','Give a caveat: "There are always risks with a new investment — I wouldn\'t overstate them."'],
'Naming the single biggest risk specifically — adoption speed, a specific threshold, a specific timeframe — and pointing to the mitigation plan is a confident, complete answer.',
'Listing all risks simultaneously dilutes the most important one. "I\'m not too worried" is a dismissal of a direct question about risk. "There are always risks" without naming yours specifically signals avoidance.'),
mcq(5,'The CEO says, "We\'ll take this to a vote — my initial view is that this is a yes." What do you say?',
'"Thank you. I\'m confident in the recommendation and the team is ready to move immediately upon approval. The proposed timeline is on the final slide."',
['Say, "Thank you so much — this means a lot to me!"','Immediately ask what the decision process and timeline will be.','Say, "If there are any concerns before the vote, I\'m happy to address them."'],
'Receiving the positive signal with calm confidence, reaffirming readiness, and pointing to the next tangible artefact — the timeline — is the executive-level close.',
'"This means a lot to me" is personal in a way that slightly undercuts your professional authority in a boardroom. Immediately asking about process when the CEO has just signalled a yes is premature. "If there are any concerns" invites further negotiation when the CEO has indicated a direction.')],
'response':resp('You built a concise, lead-with-conclusion deck, opened with an unambiguous recommendation, answered the CFO\'s financial question precisely, named the biggest risk specifically with its mitigation, and received the positive signal with confident, forward-pointing composure. Now type or say aloud the one-paragraph email you would send the executive committee the following morning summarising the next steps.',
'Excellent! Executive communication is a discipline of its own. Brevity, clarity, a clear recommendation, command of your numbers, and confident close — you showed all five. This is the communication that moves organisations.')})

levels.append({'id':68,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Dressing and Presenting Yourself',
'scenario':'You have an important in-person industry event coming up — a mix of a formal awards ceremony and a professional networking dinner. You want to make deliberate choices about how you present yourself physically, not just your outfit, but your overall presence and energy from the moment you walk in.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Choosing your outfit for the event, what is the most important principle?',
'Choose something that is one step above the expected dress code, fits you well, and makes you feel genuinely confident — not just appropriate.',
['Wear the most expensive or fashionable item you own to signal status.','Match exactly what you think everyone else will wear to blend in.','Wear something completely unexpected to stand out and be memorable.'],
'One step above the dress code, well-fitted, and personally confidence-boosting is the formula for presence-enhancing professional dress.',
'Wearing your most expensive item as a status signal is a surface strategy that often reads as trying too hard. Blending in exactly suggests no deliberate thought about impression. Being completely unexpected in a professional setting can distract from rather than enhance your credibility.'),
mcq(2,'You arrive at the event and your name tag is slightly crooked. A colleague points it out and says, "Your badge is a bit off." How do you respond?',
'Smile, fix it, and say, "Thanks — good catch. Keeping it professional to the last detail." A brief, warm recovery.',
['Ignore it: "It\'s fine — people won\'t notice."','Become self-conscious and apologise repeatedly.','Ask the event organiser to reprint a new badge immediately.'],
'A quick, warm, self-assured recovery from a small misstep — with a light touch of humour — is far more impressive than either ignoring it or being embarrassed.',
'Ignoring a correction a colleague just made looks dismissive. Apologising repeatedly about a crooked badge makes a small thing large. Asking for a reprint is a disproportionate response.'),
mcq(3,'You notice midway through the evening that you have been slouching in your chair during dinner. What do you do?',
'Quietly sit up, roll your shoulders back, and make a small decision to notice your posture for the rest of the evening without drawing attention to having corrected it.',
['Loudly excuse yourself to "straighten up": "Sorry — I\'ve been told I slouch."','Continue as you are since the evening is already half-finished.','Start overcompensating with rigid posture that looks equally unnatural.'],
'A quiet, private self-correction is self-awareness in action. No announcement needed.',
'Announcing the correction makes something minor into a self-directed observation. Continuing as you are accepts that the second half of the impression does not matter. Overcompensating with rigid posture replaces one problem with another.'),
mcq(4,'At the networking dinner, you realise you are holding your wine glass with both hands and hunching slightly. A confident presence choice would be to:',
'Move your glass to one hand, sit up slightly, and rest the other arm comfortably — projecting openness and ease.',
['Put the wine glass down entirely and fold your arms to feel more grounded.','Keep holding it with both hands since it feels comfortable and calming.','Pick up your phone so your hands have somewhere natural to go.'],
'Open posture with one hand free communicates ease, confidence, and approachability. This is what confident physical presence looks like in a social-professional setting.',
'Folding your arms while putting the glass down communicates closed posture — the opposite of presence. Holding the glass with both hands is a protective gesture. Picking up your phone is the single most presence-destroying thing you can do at a networking dinner.'),
mcq(5,'At the end of the evening, someone says to you, "You have a very calm presence — people seem to gravitate toward you." How do you receive this?',
'"That means a lot to me — it\'s something I work on. Thank you."',
['Say, "Oh I don\'t know — I\'ve been feeling quite nervous tonight."','Deflect completely: "I think people just had a good evening."','Say, "That\'s so kind — though I did spend the first hour feeling completely lost."'],
'Receiving the observation with gratitude, naming that it is intentional and something you work on, is honest and confident.',
'Admitting you felt nervous reframes the whole evening as an act. Deflecting the compliment onto the evening generally removes yourself from the observation. Saying you felt lost for the first hour makes the person wonder if the second half was real.')],
'response':resp('You made deliberate choices about dress code, recovered from a small misstep with warmth and humour, corrected your posture quietly without self-announcement, used your body language to project openness, and received a presence observation with confident honesty. Now type or say aloud three things you would do differently to prepare for a similar event next time.',
'Brilliant! How you present yourself physically is a form of communication that runs parallel to every word you speak. Fit, posture, eye contact, open body language — these are not superficial concerns. They are the frame through which everything else is received.')})

levels.append({'id':69,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Gravitas Under Pressure',
'scenario':'A major project you own has just hit a serious problem — a key supplier has failed and a client delivery is now in jeopardy. Your team is looking to you for calm direction. You are frightened and uncertain inside. But your presence in the next two hours will determine whether your team rises to meet this crisis or falls into panic.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You have just learned about the supplier failure. Your team lead is standing in front of you waiting for direction. What do you do first?',
'Take three seconds — visibly slow — then say: "Okay. Tell me exactly what we know and what we don\'t know yet." Compose your face.',
['Say, "Oh no — this is really bad. How did this happen?"','Immediately call the client to inform them before you know the full picture.','Send an urgent message to your entire team to alert them to the crisis.'],
'Three seconds of visible composure followed by an information-seeking question signals calm authority. Your face and your first response are being read by everyone.',
'"This is really bad — how did this happen" mirrors the team\'s fear rather than providing an anchor. Calling the client before you know the full picture passes on incomplete information that you cannot walk back. Alerting everyone immediately spreads the panic before you have a plan.'),
mcq(2,'You now know the facts. The supplier has a two-week delay. The client delivery is in three weeks. You have a narrow window. How do you brief your team?',
'"Here\'s where we stand: supplier failure, two-week delay, client delivery in three weeks. We have a one-week window to find an alternative or restructure the delivery. We are not in crisis if we move well in the next four hours. Here\'s what I need each of you to do."',
['"I don\'t want to cause panic but this is serious — we need to figure something out."','Give each team member the full background before telling them what to do.','Wait until you have a full solution before briefing the team so they are not worried.'],
'The calm, factual framing — here is what happened, here is the constraint, here is the window, here is what I need — is exactly how gravity sounds under pressure.',
'"I don\'t want to cause panic but this is serious" causes panic by naming panic. Giving full background before a clear ask delays action. Waiting for a full solution before briefing means lost time the team could be using.'),
mcq(3,'One team member says, "We\'re not going to be able to fix this in time." They are frightened. How do you respond?',
'"You may be right — and we\'re going to find out by trying. I need your best thinking for the next four hours, not your worst projection. Can you do that?"',
['"Don\'t say that — we need everyone to stay positive right now."','Dismiss the concern: "It\'ll be fine — stop worrying."','Say, "I hear that concern — let\'s pause and discuss it as a group."'],
'Acknowledging the possibility — "you may be right" — while redirecting to immediate action rather than projection is honest, respectful, and galvanising.',
'"Stay positive" suppresses a legitimate concern rather than addressing it. "It\'ll be fine — stop worrying" dismisses the concern and your credibility if it is not fine. Stopping to discuss the concern as a group pauses the very action the team needs to be taking.'),
mcq(4,'You have found an alternative supplier but it will cost 30% more than planned. You need to inform the client. How do you do this with gravitas?',
'"I\'m calling to give you an important update: our primary supplier failed and we have secured an alternative that guarantees your delivery date. There is a cost implication I want to discuss — but your delivery is protected. Can we speak in thirty minutes?"',
['Apologise extensively before telling the client what happened.','Send an email to avoid the difficult conversation.','Tell the client you are "working on it" without yet confirming the alternative.'],
'Leading with what is protected — the delivery date — before addressing the cost implication is the calm, confident, client-first approach that maintains trust under pressure.',
'Extensive apology before the information makes you sound less in control than you are. Email avoids a real conversation that the client deserves. "Working on it" without confirmation creates anxiety when you already have the solution.'),
mcq(5,'The crisis is resolved. The client accepted the cost implication. Your team is exhausted. How do you close the day?',
'Bring the team together briefly: "You handled that with real professionalism today. We had four hours to fix a major problem and we did it. Take a breath — and tomorrow we debrief so it doesn\'t happen again."',
['Go directly to your own desk and start the debrief analysis immediately.','Send a group message: "Great work today — well done everyone!"','Tell the team how frightened you were when you first heard the news.'],
'A brief, in-person, specific acknowledgment — naming what they did, naming the time window, giving them permission to breathe, and promising a debrief — closes the day with gravitas and care.',
'Going straight to debrief analysis without acknowledging the team ignores the emotional dimension of a crisis day. A group message is impersonal after a deeply personal shared experience. Sharing your initial fear — while honest — can undermine the authority your composure built if delivered immediately after the resolution.')],
'response':resp('You showed composure in the first three seconds, gave a calm factual briefing with a clear window and specific asks, acknowledged a team member\'s fear while redirecting to action, led with the client\'s protected delivery before the cost implication, and closed the day with specific acknowledgment and a forward debrief. Now type or say aloud the debrief agenda you would send the team the following morning.',
'Outstanding! Gravitas under pressure is the quality that separates good professionals from great leaders. It is not the absence of fear — it is the decision to lead despite it, clearly and calmly, so that everyone around you can do their best work. You did that today.')})

levels.append({'id':70,'journey_id':7,'journey_title':'Professional Presence','title':'Authentic Leadership',
'scenario':'You have been promoted to a leadership role. After three months, you are aware that your team seems slightly cautious around you — careful about what they say, conservative in their ideas. You want to be an authentic leader — someone whose team brings their best because they feel genuinely safe to do so. You decide to start with a conversation.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You open a team meeting to address the dynamic you have noticed. How do you begin?',
'"I want to be honest with you: I\'ve noticed that our conversations feel a bit careful. I don\'t want that. I want you to challenge me, disagree with me, and bring your full thinking. I\'m going to tell you why — and I\'d like to hear what\'s getting in the way for you."',
['Start with a positive agenda and hope the dynamic improves over time.','Ask your most trusted team member to brief you privately on what people think.','Set up an anonymous feedback tool to find out what people are experiencing.'],
'Naming what you have observed directly and honestly, stating what you want instead, and inviting their perspective is authentic leadership in action.',
'A positive agenda without addressing the dynamic you noticed allows the cautiousness to continue. Getting a private briefing from one trusted person introduces partisanship. An anonymous tool is useful but removes the personal accountability of a direct conversation.'),
mcq(2,'A team member says, "Honestly, we\'re not always sure what you want — sometimes you say you\'re open to ideas and then the decision has clearly already been made." How do you respond?',
'"That\'s a fair challenge — and I think you\'re right that it has happened. If I\'ve made a decision and I\'m sharing it as a decision, I need to be clearer about that. Thank you for naming it."',
['Defend yourself: "I do listen — I can give you specific examples where I changed direction based on team input."','Say, "I appreciate the honesty, but decisions have to be made."','Dismiss it: "I don\'t think that\'s quite accurate — we always discuss things.'],
'Accepting the specific observation without defending, naming the specific behaviour change you will make — "clearer about when something is a decision" — and thanking them is genuine and concrete.',
'Defending with specific counter-examples turns the conversation into a debate rather than an acknowledgment. "Decisions have to be made" is true but dismisses the specific feedback. "I don\'t think that\'s quite accurate" denies an experience that was just shared openly.'),
mcq(3,'You want to model the intellectual courage you are asking for. How do you do this explicitly?',
'"Let me share a decision I made last month that I\'m now not sure was right — [specific example]. I\'d like to hear what you think I should have done differently."',
['Tell the team about a past failure at a previous company to build vulnerability without personal risk.','Share something personal about your life to create intimacy and trust.','Say, "I\'m always learning — I know I\'m not perfect" without a specific example.'],
'Sharing a recent, specific, real decision you are questioning — within this team\'s experience — is authentic vulnerability that models exactly the intellectual courage you are asking for.',
'A past failure at a previous company is safe and therefore not quite the same as current vulnerability. Personal life sharing creates intimacy of a different kind from professional intellectual courage. "I\'m not perfect" without a specific example is a statement, not a model.'),
mcq(4,'A team member challenges the decision you just shared and says you made the wrong call. How do you respond?',
'Listen fully, nod, and say: "Walk me through your reasoning." When they do, say: "I think you\'re right. I should have [specific alternative]."',
['Defend the decision now that it is under challenge.','Say, "I appreciate that view — I\'ll take it on board."','Change the subject once you have heard their reasoning.'],
'Actually changing your stated view when someone makes a strong argument — and naming specifically what you would have done differently — demonstrates that you meant what you said about being open to challenge.',
'Defending the decision after openly inviting challenge is a credibility collapse. "I\'ll take it on board" is the phrase that teams have learned to mean "thank you, I hear you, moving on." Changing the subject after receiving a strong argument is a silent retreat.'),
mcq(5,'At the end of the meeting, a team member says, "That was different." How do you close?',
'"Yes — and I want it to stay different. I\'m going to hold myself accountable to what I said today. If you notice me reverting, please call it. That permission is real."',
['Say, "Good — glad we got there. Same time next week!"','Say, "I hope we can keep this energy up from now on."','Nothing — let the moment speak for itself.'],
'Naming the change, committing accountability, and explicitly repeating the permission to call you out — "that permission is real" — turns a single meeting into a genuine leadership pledge.',
'"Same time next week" treats a breakthrough moment like a regular agenda item. "I hope we can keep this energy up" is vague and places the responsibility on the team. Saying nothing lets the moment pass without the explicit commitment that makes it more than a good meeting.')],
'response':resp('You opened with honest self-observation, accepted specific feedback without defending, modelled intellectual courage with a real recent decision, changed your stated view when challenged with strong reasoning, and closed with an explicit, accountable leadership pledge. Now type or say aloud the one-sentence leadership principle you would write down for yourself after this meeting.',
'Remarkable! Authentic leadership is the rarest and most valuable kind. It requires courage to be honest about what you have noticed, humility to accept feedback, and real vulnerability to model what you are asking for. You did all three today. That is how teams begin to bring their best.')})
